The quintessential holiday breakfast and party showstopper. Tender refrigerated buttermilk biscuits are cut into bite-sized morsels, tossed in fragrant cinnamon sugar, and baked in a Bundt pan under a rich glaze of melted butter and brown sugar. Inverting the pan reveals a gleaming, sticky caramel cascade.

*Adapted from [Self Proclaimed Foodie](https://selfproclaimedfoodie.com/grannys-monkey-bread/).*

## Ingredients

- 24 oz refrigerated buttermilk biscuit dough (three 8-oz cans or two 12-oz cans)
- 1 cup granulated sugar
- 2 teaspoons ground cinnamon (or pumpkin pie spice)
- 1 cup (2 sticks) unsalted butter
- ½ cup packed dark or light brown sugar
- 1 tablespoon softened butter (for heavily greasing pan)

## Instructions

1. **Grease the Bundt Pan Thoroughly:** Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C). Using your fingers or a paper towel, smear 1 tablespoon of softened butter liberally all over the inside of a 10-to-12-cup Bundt pan, paying special attention to the bottom flutes and central cone.
2. **Portion the Biscuit Dough:** Pop open the cans of refrigerated biscuit dough. Separate the biscuits and use a chef's knife or kitchen shears to cut each biscuit into 4 equal triangular quarters.
3. **Coat in Cinnamon-Sugar:** In a large mixing bowl or a gallon-sized zip-top bag, combine 1 cup of granulated sugar and 2 teaspoons of ground cinnamon. Toss the biscuit pieces into the cinnamon sugar in two or three batches, turning until every morsel is coated in spiced sugar.
4. **Arrange in the Pan:** Drop the coated dough balls loosely into the prepared Bundt pan, distributing them evenly around the central tube without packing them down.
5. **Make the Brown Sugar Caramel Glaze:** In a small saucepan over medium heat, combine 1 cup (2 sticks) of butter, ½ cup packed brown sugar, and ½ cup of the excess cinnamon-sugar mixture left over from coating the biscuits (discard any remaining sugar). Heat, stirring frequently with a whisk, just until the butter melts and the mixture comes to a gentle simmer. Remove immediately from the heat as soon as the sugars dissolve into a silky, glossy syrup.
6. **Glaze the Dough:** Slowly drizzle the hot caramel syrup evenly over the biscuit pieces in the Bundt pan, letting it seep down between all the nooks and crannies.
7. **Bake to Golden Perfection:** Place the Bundt pan on the center oven rack (place a baking sheet on the rack below to catch any rogue drips). Bake for 28 to 32 minutes, until the top is deep golden brown, crusty, and the biscuits in the center have baked through.
8. **Rest and Invert:** Allow the pan to rest on a cooling rack for exactly 5 minutes. (Do not let it sit longer, or the cooling caramel will cement the bread to the pan). Place a large rimmed serving platter upside down over the Bundt pan. Using oven mitts, firmly hold the pan and platter together and invert in one swift, confident motion. Let the pan rest inverted for 1 minute so all the warm caramel drips onto the bread, then lift the pan away. Serve immediately.

## Notes

- **Nutty or Fruity Additions:** Scatter ½ cup of toasted chopped pecans or walnuts and a handful of dried cranberries or raisins among the biscuit layers for added texture.
- **Serving:** Best enjoyed fresh and warm with fingers, pulling off individual gooey, caramel-drenched bites.
